---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
import { destinations } from '../lib/data';
import { IDEAL_HIGH } from '../lib/score';
import { breadcrumbLd } from '../lib/seo';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'How we rate', href: '/about/' },
];
const adjusted = destinations.filter((d) => Object.keys(d.adjust).length > 0);
---

<Base
  title="How We Rate Destinations"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} scores the weather for every destination and month: data sources, formula and seasonal adjustments.`}
  jsonLd={[breadcrumbLd(crumbs, Astro.site)]}
>
  <div class="container">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <article class="prose section-tight">
      <h1>How we rate destinations</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Every month of every destination gets a weather score from 0 to 100. Here is exactly how it is calculated — no
        sponsored placements, no guesswork.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Temperatures, rainfall, rainy days and sunshine</b> are climate normals — usually 30-year averages for
          1991–2020 — published by national meteorological services (such as IPMA in Portugal, the Thai Meteorological
          Department or the Japan Meteorological Agency). We take them from the "Climate data" tables in each city's
          English Wikipedia article, which cite those services, and link the source on every destination page.
        </li>
        <li>Where a table has no rainfall figures, we use NASA's POWER climatology (MERRA-2, 2001–2020).</li>
        <li>
          <b>Daily budgets</b> are our own estimates of typical {SITE.priceYear} costs per person, excluding flights: a
          "budget" day means hostels or guesthouses, street food and public transport; "mid-range" means a comfortable
          hotel, restaurant meals and some paid activities.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The weather score</h2>
      <p>The score blends two parts — <b>temperature (55%)</b> and <b>rainfall (45%)</b>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Temperature</b> starts at 100 when the average daily high sits in a comfortable range: {IDEAL_HIGH.temperate[0]}–{IDEAL_HIGH.temperate[1]}°C
          for most cities, {IDEAL_HIGH.tropical[0]}–{IDEAL_HIGH.tropical[1]}°C for tropical beach and city destinations, and
          {IDEAL_HIGH.highland[0]}–{IDEAL_HIGH.highland[1]}°C for high-altitude places like Cusco or Mexico City. We subtract 9
          points per degree below the range and 10–11 per degree above it, plus up to 20 points for near-freezing nights.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Rainfall</b> scores 100 up to 40 mm a month, then falls steadily: about 50 at 150 mm, 20 at 300 mm and close to
          zero in the wettest monsoon months.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Seasonal adjustments.</b> Some things climate averages miss: Chiang Mai's smoke season, Caribbean hurricanes,
          typhoons in central Vietnam, monsoon closures in Goa or the Inca Trail closing in February. For
          {adjusted.length} destinations we subtract points in the affected months and say why on the page.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the ratings mean</h2>
      <div class="legend">
        <span><i data-rating="great"></i><b>Great (80–100)</b> — about as good as it gets</span>
        <span><i data-rating="good"></i><b>Good (65–79)</b> — a solid choice with minor trade-offs</span>
        <span><i data-rating="fair"></i><b>Fair (50–64)</b> — noticeable heat, cold or rain</span>
        <span><i data-rating="poor"></i><b>Not ideal (under 50)</b> — consider another month</span>
      </div>

      <h2>Limits</h2>
      <p>
        Averages describe typical conditions, not the forecast for your dates, and a score can't capture everything — crowds,
        prices, festivals or personal taste. That's why every page also lists events, seasonal notes and budgets. If you
        spot an error, we'd love to fix it.
      </p>

      <h2>How we make money</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is free. Some links are affiliate links to booking sites: if you book through them we may earn a small
        commission at no extra cost to you. Partners never influence scores or rankings. <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Read
        the full disclosure</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .legend {
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: start;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    gap: 10px;
  }
</style>
